      "As of 28 September the split has a published court judgment and the Department's reasons are on the record in the government's own words. On 25 September the D.C. Circuit decided Anthropic PBC v. United States Department of War, No. 26-1049, consolidated with 26-1162, argued on 19 May. Circuit Judge Katsas wrote for the court, Circuit Judge Henderson dissented, and CNBC and Courthouse News both report the vote as 2-1 with Circuit Judge Rao joining. The opinion states that the Department excluded Claude from its supply chain under the Federal Acquisition Supply Chain Security Act of 2018 after Anthropic refused to relax contractual prohibitions on the use of Claude for lethal autonomous warfare or domestic surveillance, and that the Department had ample support for its conclusion that continued integration of Claude presented a statutorily covered national-security risk. It records that Under Secretary for Research and Engineering Emil Michael cited Anthropic's refusal to allow all lawful uses and its ability to alter system guardrails and model weights. Anthropic's due-process and First Amendment claims were rejected.",
      "What the ruling does not settle is the thing this storyline tracks. The opinion never mentions GenAI.mil, the Maven Smart System, or any model that replaced Claude; the transition status rests where it did, on one official's word. Henderson's dissent argues the case turns entirely on the scope of the residual clause and warns that a future contractor faces a choice between agreeing to the Secretary's demands or being designated a national security threat. An Anthropic spokesperson said the company is considering all options, including further review, and noted that another federal court has held a parallel designation unlawful; Breaking Defense reports the remaining routes are a discretionary en banc rehearing or a Supreme Court petition. No department statement or filing confirming completion of the transition has been published."
    ],
